{"archetypes":[{"name":"export-wiring-completeness","focus_area":"correctness","weight":0.6,"rationale":"The plan's 4-site export wiring description is the highest-risk correctness gap. Tests 1 and 2 (lines 1737-1755, 1788-1806) do not stub python3 via PATH, so after refactor they need DEDUP_PLAN_LINES_PY pointing to the real extracted script path before the bash -c invocation; check whether the plan specifies this assignment and whether the inner export lists at lines 1752 and 1803 are updated to include it (omission causes set -u unbound failure under eval-isolation). Tests 3 and 4 (lines 1826-1846, 1868-1887) currently stub python3 via PATH=$STUB:$PATH; after refactor the stub must target DEDUP_PLAN_LINES_PY directly rather than python3, so the plan must replace the stub creation pattern, not layer a new export on top of the existing PATH prefix. If both stubs coexist, the dedup-python-failed test exercises the PATH stub rather than the script-path stub, leaving the PYWRAP recursion risk untested. Confirm whether the plan specifies stub-replacement or stub-addition at sites 3 and 4, and whether the new integration test's REAL_PYTHON capture happens before any PATH mutation.","prompt_body":"The plan adds DEDUP_PLAN_LINES_PY to both the outer export block and the inner bash -c export list at all 4 eval-isolation test sites in skills/design/scripts/test-plan-review-loop.sh. Tests 1 and 2 (lines 1737-1755, 1788-1806) do not manipulate PATH and use real python3, so they need DEDUP_PLAN_LINES_PY set to the real extracted script path before the bash -c invocation; check whether the plan specifies this assignment and whether the inner export lists at lines 1752 and 1803 are updated to include it (omission causes set -u unbound failure under eval-isolation). Tests 3 and 4 (lines 1826-1846, 1868-1887) currently stub python3 via PATH=\"$STUB:$PATH\"; after refactor the stub must target DEDUP_PLAN_LINES_PY directly, not python3, so the plan must replace the stub creation pattern rather than layer a new export on top of the existing PATH prefix -- if both stubs coexist the dedup-python-failed test would exercise the PATH stub rather than the script-path stub, leaving the PYWRAP recursion risk untested. Confirm whether the plan specifies stub-replacement or stub-addition at sites 3 and 4, and whether the new integration test's REAL_PYTHON capture happens before any PATH mutation. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"documentation-invariant-accuracy","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":0.4,"rationale":"The plan introduces dedup-plan-lines.md documenting fence-model divergence from parse-plan-commands.awk. The code at plan-review-loop.sh lines 543-574 shows balanced-fence interior lines are placed in in_fence_lines and ARE subject to dedup (the fenced-count==1 assertion at test line 1768 confirms fenced duplicates collapse), while the KEY PLAN EDGE CASES note says the doc must not claim fenced regions are protected. However, the doc must also correctly represent the asymmetry between fence interior lines (deduped), heading-reset behavior for in-fence headings (lines 568-569 reset prev_key=None even inside fences), and the Constraints-protection boundary (line 571 uses not-in-fence as its guard). A documentation draft that conflates fence-content dedup suppression with Constraints suppression would produce a false invariant, and a draft that omits the in-fence heading-reset side effect would leave a correctness gap undocumented.","prompt_body":"The plan introduces skills/design/scripts/dedup-plan-lines.md documenting the fence-model behavior and its divergence from parse-plan-commands.awk. Verify the plan's documentation invariant claims against the code at plan-review-loop.sh lines 543-576: balanced-fence interior lines (tracked via in_fence_lines, lines 543-561) ARE deduped just like plain lines; the in-fence heading-reset at lines 568-569 fires even inside fences, clearing prev_key regardless of fence state; and the Constraints-protection guard at line 571 uses 'inside_constraints and not in_fence', meaning fenced content inside a Constraints section is NOT protected. Check whether the plan's documentation accurately distinguishes these three behaviors and does not conflate fence-content dedup bypass with Constraints suppression, which would produce a false invariant. Also check whether the plan specifies that dedup-plan-lines.md must not claim fence markers themselves are skipped from dedup (they pass through the key-comparison path at lines 570-575 and could in principle be deduplicated if identical fence marker lines appeared consecutively). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
